# A meta-package whose only job is to depend on the firmware this device needs,
# so that firmware can never be reported as an orphan.
#
# Why this exists: trimming the `linux-firmware` meta-package (to drop ~840 MB
# of blobs for hardware a CM5 cannot have) left the kept subpackages with
# nothing depending on them. Omarchy runs `omarchy-update-orphan-pkgs` on every
# update, offering to `pacman -Rns` anything orphaned - and a user who accepts
# loses their wifi and Bluetooth firmware, on a device whose only network is
# wifi.
#
# Marking them explicit with `pacman -D --asexplicit` fixes it in the image, but
# was observed NOT to survive on a real device: after flashing and updating,
# they showed up as orphans again. Rather than keep chasing whatever re-marks
# them, this makes the question moot - a package depending on them means they
# are never orphans, whatever their install reason says.
pkgname=uconsole-firmware
pkgver=1.0.0
pkgrel=1
pkgdesc='Pins the firmware the ClockworkPi uConsole CM5 needs so it is never orphaned'
arch=('any')
url='https://github.com/tiandklerk/omarchy-uconsole'
license=('MIT')

depends=(
  # The CM5 radio: brcmfmac wifi and the CYW43455 Bluetooth blob. Losing this
  # costs the only network interface the device has.
  'linux-firmware-broadcom'
  # Licence text the other firmware packages reference.
  'linux-firmware-whence'
  # Plausible USB peripherals: realtek covers most USB ethernet and wifi
  # dongles, cirrus covers audio codecs.
  'linux-firmware-realtek'
  'linux-firmware-cirrus'
  'linux-firmware-other'
)

package() {
  install -Dm644 /dev/stdin "$pkgdir/usr/share/doc/$pkgname/README" <<'DOC'
This package installs no files. It exists only so that the firmware packages it
depends on are never reported as orphans by `pacman -Qtdq`, and therefore never
offered for removal by omarchy-update-orphan-pkgs.

Removing this package will orphan the uConsole's wifi and Bluetooth firmware.
DOC
}
